1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,318
A marble hits a still
marble, and stops dead.

2
00:00:03,318 --> 00:00:06,231
A ball hits a wall, and
bounces straight back.

3
00:00:06,631 --> 00:00:10,360
And a heavy ball can send a light
one off faster than it came in.

4
00:00:10,810 --> 00:00:14,258
Aarav has a desk toy, a
row of hanging steel balls.

5
00:00:14,258 --> 00:00:16,999
Swing one in, and exactly one flies out.

6
00:00:17,349 --> 00:00:19,291
Swing two, and two fly out.

7
00:00:19,291 --> 00:00:21,620
Why never one ball, twice as fast?

8
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:24,225
One kind of collision
explains all of this.

9
00:00:24,825 --> 00:00:28,407
In every collision, the total
momentum stays the same.

10
00:00:28,407 --> 00:00:30,094
The pushes are internal.

11
00:00:30,444 --> 00:00:35,132
In an elastic collision, one more thing
is saved: the total kinetic energy.

12
00:00:35,532 --> 00:00:39,616
The balls squash a little, like
a spring, then spring back.

13
00:00:39,616 --> 00:00:42,574
Nothing is lost as heat, sound or a dent.

14
00:00:43,174 --> 00:00:44,694
Our motion notebook.

15
00:00:44,694 --> 00:00:48,786
Ball A, two kilograms, rolls
at three metres per second.

16
00:00:48,786 --> 00:00:51,847
Ball B, also two kilograms, is at rest.

17
00:00:52,197 --> 00:00:53,129
You predict.

18
00:00:53,129 --> 00:00:56,446
After the hit, does A
stop dead, or bounce back?

19
00:00:56,446 --> 00:00:57,676
Pause and decide.

20
00:01:00,876 --> 00:01:01,789
It stops dead!

21
00:01:01,789 --> 00:01:03,081
B moves off at three.

22
00:01:03,081 --> 00:01:04,526
The balls swap velocities.

23
00:01:04,926 --> 00:01:07,540
Momentum, six before and six after.

24
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:10,750
Kinetic energy, nine
joules before and after.

25
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:12,222
Slow-mo replay.

26
00:01:12,222 --> 00:01:14,038
Freeze at the biggest squash.

27
00:01:14,388 --> 00:01:16,907
Both move together at one point five.

28
00:01:16,907 --> 00:01:19,935
Kinetic energy dips to
four point five joules.

29
00:01:19,935 --> 00:01:22,254
The rest is stored in the squash.

30
00:01:22,754 --> 00:01:25,228
Then it springs back,
and every joule returns.

31
00:01:25,228 --> 00:01:27,963
The total on the graph
comes back to the same line.

32
00:01:28,563 --> 00:01:30,305
Now A is four kilograms.

33
00:01:30,305 --> 00:01:31,899
A keeps going at one.

34
00:01:31,899 --> 00:01:33,492
B shoots off at four.

35
00:01:33,892 --> 00:01:34,792
Flip it.

36
00:01:34,612 --> 00:01:36,732
A is two kilograms, B is four.

37
00:01:36,732 --> 00:01:39,824
A bounces back at one,
and B moves off at two.

38
00:01:40,224 --> 00:01:41,729
Three different results.

39
00:01:41,729 --> 00:01:44,804
Momentum and kinetic energy
both kept, every time.

40
00:01:45,404 --> 00:01:47,664
Now the formulas, one step at a time.

41
00:01:47,664 --> 00:01:49,140
Ball two starts at rest.

42
00:01:49,490 --> 00:01:50,390
Step one.

43
00:01:50,359 --> 00:01:54,755
Momentum: m one u one equals
m one v one plus m two v two.

44
00:01:55,505 --> 00:01:56,405
Step two.

45
00:01:56,389 --> 00:02:01,562
Kinetic energy: half m one u one
squared equals half m one v one squared,

46
00:02:01,562 --> 00:02:03,901
plus half m two v two squared.

47
00:02:04,651 --> 00:02:05,551
Step three.

48
00:02:05,483 --> 00:02:08,559
Group ball one's terms on
the left, in both lines.

49
00:02:09,309 --> 00:02:10,209
Step four.

50
00:02:10,166 --> 00:02:12,197
Divide one line by the other.

51
00:02:12,197 --> 00:02:14,395
u one plus v one equals v two.

52
00:02:14,395 --> 00:02:17,648
So they move apart as fast
as they came together.

53
00:02:18,398 --> 00:02:19,405
Step five.

54
00:02:19,405 --> 00:02:21,731
Put that back into step one.

55
00:02:21,731 --> 00:02:27,076
v one is m one minus m two, over
m one plus m two, times u one.

56
00:02:27,076 --> 00:02:31,646
v two is two m one over m
one plus m two, times u one.

57
00:02:32,396 --> 00:02:36,950
Our three runs give zero and three,
one and four, minus one and two.

58
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:38,300
Just like the sim.

59
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:39,822
Remember this.

60
00:02:39,822 --> 00:02:44,167
In an elastic collision, the bodies move
apart as fast as they came together.

61
00:02:44,867 --> 00:02:46,985
Now Aarav's puzzle, link by link.

62
00:02:46,985 --> 00:02:48,453
Each ball has mass m.

63
00:02:48,803 --> 00:02:51,215
Two balls come in at speed v.

64
00:02:51,215 --> 00:02:52,740
Momentum two m v.

65
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:54,902
Kinetic energy m v squared.

66
00:02:55,302 --> 00:02:57,930
Suppose one ball flew out at two v.

67
00:02:57,930 --> 00:02:59,510
Momentum, two m v.

68
00:02:59,510 --> 00:03:00,485
That works.

69
00:03:00,885 --> 00:03:04,778
But its kinetic energy is
half m times two v squared.

70
00:03:04,778 --> 00:03:06,189
Two m v squared.

71
00:03:06,189 --> 00:03:07,089
Double!

72
00:03:07,311 --> 00:03:09,671
Energy cannot appear from nothing.

73
00:03:09,671 --> 00:03:12,598
So two balls must fly out, at speed v.

74
00:03:13,198 --> 00:03:14,228
Side by side.

75
00:03:14,228 --> 00:03:16,591
A very heavy ball hits a light one.

76
00:03:16,591 --> 00:03:18,402
The heavy one barely slows.

77
00:03:18,402 --> 00:03:21,395
The light one leaves at
nearly twice its speed.

78
00:03:21,795 --> 00:03:24,109
A light ball hits a very heavy one.

79
00:03:24,109 --> 00:03:28,251
It bounces back at nearly the same
speed, like a ball off a wall.

80
00:03:28,851 --> 00:03:29,830
Now you try.

81
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:34,924
A bus at ten metres per second hits a
still rubber ball, head on and elastically.

82
00:03:34,924 --> 00:03:38,202
Does the ball leave at
about ten, or about twenty?

83
00:03:38,202 --> 00:03:39,418
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:42,618 --> 00:03:43,518
About twenty!

85
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:47,818
The bus keeps nearly ten, and they must
separate as fast as they approached.

86
00:03:48,468 --> 00:03:50,158
Now, one worked example.

87
00:03:50,508 --> 00:03:55,380
A three kilogram block slides at five
metres per second on smooth ice into

88
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:57,566
a two kilogram block at rest.

89
00:03:57,566 --> 00:03:59,094
The hit is elastic.

90
00:03:59,094 --> 00:04:00,665
Find both velocities.

91
00:04:01,165 --> 00:04:04,669
v one is three minus two,
over five, times five.

92
00:04:04,669 --> 00:04:07,217
One metre per second, still forward.

93
00:04:07,967 --> 00:04:10,852
v two is six over five, times five.

94
00:04:10,852 --> 00:04:12,630
Six metres per second.

95
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:14,280
Check.

96
00:04:14,025 --> 00:04:17,858
Momentum, fifteen before,
and three plus twelve after.

97
00:04:17,858 --> 00:04:21,587
Kinetic energy, thirty seven
point five, both times.

98
00:04:22,337 --> 00:04:25,630
And six minus one is five,
the speed of approach.

99
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:27,742
Now, the exam trap.

100
00:04:28,092 --> 00:04:30,336
Many students think elastic
bodies always swap velocities.

101
00:04:30,736 --> 00:04:33,354
They swap only when the masses are equal.

102
00:04:33,354 --> 00:04:36,604
With four and two kilograms,
A kept moving at one.

103
00:04:37,254 --> 00:04:38,374
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:38,374 --> 00:04:41,735
In numericals on final velocities,
energy passed on, and the heavy and

105
00:04:41,735 --> 00:04:42,635
light limits.

106
00:04:43,102 --> 00:04:44,303
Exam level now.

107
00:04:44,303 --> 00:04:48,760
Ball A, mass m, hits ball B at
rest, head on and elastically.

108
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:52,118
A keeps moving forward at
one third of its speed.

109
00:04:52,118 --> 00:04:53,858
What is the mass of B?

110
00:04:53,858 --> 00:04:55,110
Pause and solve.

111
00:05:00,510 --> 00:05:02,020
The answer is A.

112
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:03,050
m by two.

113
00:05:03,550 --> 00:05:08,032
v one equals m minus M,
over m plus M, times u.

114
00:05:08,532 --> 00:05:10,431
Set this to u by three.

115
00:05:10,431 --> 00:05:13,334
Three m minus three M equals m plus M.

116
00:05:13,834 --> 00:05:17,490
So two m equals four M, and M is m by two.

117
00:05:18,090 --> 00:05:19,630
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:19,630 --> 00:05:21,213
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:24,413 --> 00:05:26,352
Can you answer these without help?

120
00:05:26,752 --> 00:05:27,652
One.

121
00:05:27,177 --> 00:05:30,323
Why does only one steel ball
fly out when one swings in?

122
00:05:31,223 --> 00:05:32,123
Two.

123
00:05:31,512 --> 00:05:32,848
When do two bodies swap velocities?

124
00:05:33,748 --> 00:05:34,648
Three.

125
00:05:34,322 --> 00:05:37,857
A ball hits a wall elastically
at five metres per second.

126
00:05:37,857 --> 00:05:39,700
How fast does it come back?

127
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:43,910
Next time: two trolleys
crash and stick together.

128
00:05:43,910 --> 00:05:46,039
Where does the lost energy go?

129
00:05:46,039 --> 00:05:47,986
Perfectly inelastic collision.
